# Uplink relay: every variable it reads (docs/SELF_HOSTING.md).
#
# NOTHING HERE IS REQUIRED. With this file empty, or with no file at all, the relay runs: the hub (/hub), pairings
# (/relay/...), Files spaces (/files), circle invite words (/word) and its own Marketplace (/market) all work with
# their defaults. Each group below switches on one more thing, and says what is off without it.
#
# One NAME=value a line, no quotes, no spaces. Lines starting with # are ignored. Keep the filled-in copy where only
# root (or the relay's own user) can read it: some values are secrets.
#
# Used by: systemd's EnvironmentFile= (deploy/ghost-relay.service), docker compose's env_file: (deploy/compose.yaml),
# or `set -a; . ./relay.env; set +a` before running the program by hand.

# --- Where it listens ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The port, where --port isn't given (8787). --host and --data are command-line flags, not variables.
# PORT=8787

# --- The app in a browser --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional. A folder holding the app built for a browser (`node tool/build-web.mjs` in the app's repository writes
# build/web), served as plain files under /app/, each answer with the app's headers (its policy among them). Without it
# there is no /app/. A build that names no hub dials /hub beside its page, so whatever stands in front of the relay must
# pass /app/ on to it beside /hub. The same as --web.
# GHOST_WEB_DIR=/opt/ghost-relay/web
# Optional. Where the app in a browser is served from an address of its own instead (a build made with
# `node tool/build-web.mjs --hub wss://<this relay>/hub`, on a host of its own): that address, as a browser names it
# (https://host, no slash after). Its page may then read the answers to the two things it sends this relay, a report
# (POST /market/abuse) and feedback (POST /feedback), and nothing else. Without it the page at that address can send
# neither; a page served by this relay's own /app/ needs nothing set.
# GHOST_APP_ORIGIN=https://app.relay.example.com

# --- Where it is reached from outside ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Your relay's public https address, no trailing slash. Only used as the address a network sign-in (Discord, X)
# comes back to, so it matters only with those set below. Set it anyway: its built-in default is not yours.
GHOST_PUBLIC_URL=https://relay.example.com

# Every host name your hub is reached at, commas between (with a port only where it is not 443): relay.example.com,
# www.relay.example.com, and any other name pointed at it, old ones still in use included. With them the hub takes a
# sign-in bound to the name the app dialled, and from an account that has signed in so, no other: a sign-in made for
# another relay can't be passed on to yours. List every name, since an app that dialled one not listed is refused, and
# keep each registered to you for as long as it is listed. Set it only once your people's apps sign in v2
# (relay/README.md, "Switching v2 on"). Left empty, sign-ins are taken as they always were.
# GHOST_HUB_NAMES=relay.example.com,www.relay.example.com

# --- Keeping what is held across a restart ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Packets for people who are away, and what rooms keep for whoever joins later, live in memory, and a restart loses
# them. With GHOST_HUB_KEEP_HELD=1 they are kept on this machine's disk too, under the data folder's held/, still
# sealed, with who each is for and who sent it, for up to a week (README.md, "What it keeps"), and a restart loses at
# most its last fifth of a second. The disk holds at most about twice what is held plus 24 MB.
# GHOST_HUB_KEEP_HELD=1

# --- How much every connection may hold together ------------------------------------------------------------------
# What all connections hold at once (a message half come in, what waits for a peer to read) is counted against
# GHOST_WIRE_MB megabytes. A connection is ended for it only when more than GHOST_WIRE_EACH_MB waits to go out to it
# for GHOST_WIRE_STALL_S seconds on end, or a message coming in hasn't grown for GHOST_WIRE_IN_STALL_S. Past
# GHOST_WIRE_MB together, those stalled longest go first, then those holding the most. The defaults suit a relay with
# 300 MB; give more where it has more.
# GHOST_WIRE_MB=64
# GHOST_WIRE_EACH_MB=8
# GHOST_WIRE_STALL_S=10
# GHOST_WIRE_IN_STALL_S=20

# --- Talking to other relays ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Off unless GHOST_FED says otherwise, and off this relay is an island: it dials nobody, links with no relay, and
# people here reach only people here through it. On, a friend on another relay (added as @name@their.relay) is
# reached through this one: the two relays pass each other the sealed packets (relay/README.md, "Other relays").
#   off   (the default)
#   open  with any relay that proves its key, but those in GHOST_FED_BLOCK
#   list  only with the relays in GHOST_FED_ALLOW
# GHOST_PUBLIC_HUB is this relay's own hub address as the world reaches it. Set it with GHOST_FED: other relays
# dial it to check this relay is who it says, and people here are reached at it. Without it this relay can ask other
# relays who someone is, but can't pass its people's packets to them, and takes no other relay's hello.
# GHOST_FED_ALLOW and GHOST_FED_BLOCK are hosts (relay.example.org, or with a port) or relays' names (the 32 hex
# digits a relay prints as it starts: "this one is ..."), commas between.
# GHOST_FED=off
# GHOST_PUBLIC_HUB=wss://relay.example.com/hub
# GHOST_FED_ALLOW=
# GHOST_FED_BLOCK=
# What one linked relay may send here: packets a minute, bytes a day, and how many usernames it may ask after an hour.
# A relay is counted by the address this relay has checked it at; every relay with no address shares one allowance.
# Each of your own people may use a quarter of the packets and an eighth of the bytes for what they send to others.
# GHOST_FED_PACKETS_A_MINUTE=1200
# GHOST_FED_BYTES_A_DAY=536870912
# GHOST_FED_LOOKUPS_AN_HOUR=600
# Other relays are dialled at wss:// on port 443 and nowhere else, and never at an address on this machine or its
# own network. GHOST_FED_PORTS names more ports that may be dialled, commas between; GHOST_FED_PLAIN=1 lets ws://
# (no TLS) be dialled too, which you want only between machines you run yourself.
# GHOST_FED_PORTS=
# GHOST_FED_PLAIN=0
# For a relay with no address of its own (one behind a home router, or hosted from the app): the public relay it
# dials and stays linked to, through which its people are reached. Leave GHOST_PUBLIC_HUB unset with it.
# GHOST_FED_VIA=
# On a public relay: which relays with no address may stay linked to this one, their people reached through it and
# their packets passed on to other relays under this relay's name and allowance. Off unless you say open, or name
# the relays (their 32 hex digits), commas between. It is for relays hosted from the app.
# GHOST_FED_STANDING=off

# --- The admin page (Settings › Admin in the app) ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional, and of little use on your relay: the app shows Settings › Admin only to one built-in username, in
# developer mode. Without GHOST_ADMIN_USER and GHOST_ADMIN_HASH nobody can sign in to it, and nothing else changes.
# GHOST_ADMIN_HASH is "salt:hash" (32 hex, a colon, 64 hex), as the repo's tool/admin-password.mjs writes it.
# GHOST_ADMIN_NAMES is the Uplink usernames (on this relay) allowed to try, commas between. It is set empty here on
# purpose: its built-in default is someone else's username.
# GHOST_ADMIN_USER=
# GHOST_ADMIN_HASH=
GHOST_ADMIN_NAMES=

# --- Calls through a TURN server (coturn) ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional. Without them a call that can't go straight between the two devices fails instead of going through the
# relay. GHOST_TURN_SECRET is coturn's static-auth-secret (32 to 128 letters, digits, - or _); GHOST_TURN_URLS is
# where coturn is, as WebRTC names it, commas between; GHOST_TURN_TTL is how long a handed-out password lasts, in
# seconds (86400). Both SECRET and URLS, or neither. Sample coturn config: deploy/turnserver.conf.example.
# GHOST_TURN_SECRET=
# GHOST_TURN_URLS=turn:relay.example.com:3478?transport=udp,turn:relay.example.com:3478?transport=tcp
# GHOST_TURN_TTL=86400

# --- Link helpers for direct links between devices -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional. The apps also make direct links between a person's own devices and their friends' devices. A link helper
# (an iroh-relay server, not this program) helps two devices find each other across networks and carries their sealed
# traffic where no straight path can be made; it sees which devices link and how much they send, never who they are
# or what is said. GHOST_LINK_HELPERS is where yours are: https addresses, commas between, 4 at most, 200 characters
# each at most. The relay hands them to each app as it signs in. Without it devices link only on one network.
# GHOST_LINK_HELPERS=https://link.relay.example.com

# --- Its own Marketplace ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The apps read the Marketplace from Uplink's own relay whatever relay they use, so yours is never asked.
# 0 closes it (GET /market says it is closed, everything else answers 503). Leave it at 0 unless you are working on
# the Marketplace itself.
GHOST_MARKET_ON=0
# Its limits, each with a default where unset (relay/lib/market.dart MarketConfig, relay/README.md):
# GHOST_MARKET_MAX_PACKAGE=262144
# GHOST_MARKET_MAX_SHOT=1048576
# GHOST_MARKET_MAX_SHOTS=4
# GHOST_MARKET_MAX_BODY=6291456
# GHOST_MARKET_TOTAL=1073741824
# GHOST_MARKET_SUBMISSIONS_A_DAY=5
# GHOST_MARKET_SUBMISSIONS_PER_ADDRESS=3
# GHOST_MARKET_OPEN_PER_DEVELOPER=3
# GHOST_MARKET_OPEN_PER_ADDRESS=3
# GHOST_MARKET_OPEN_TOTAL=200
# GHOST_MARKET_OPEN_UNVERIFIED=20
# GHOST_MARKET_NAME_AGE_DAYS=7
# GHOST_MARKET_PLUGINS_PER_DEVELOPER=20
# GHOST_MARKET_MAX_PLUGINS=1000
# GHOST_MARKET_BYTES_PER_DEVELOPER=25165824
# GHOST_MARKET_SIGNED_AN_HOUR=300
# GHOST_MARKET_SIGNED_PER_ADDRESS=60
# GHOST_MARKET_VERIFY_A_SECOND=20
# GHOST_MARKET_BODIES_IN_FLIGHT=3
# GHOST_MARKET_READS_PER_MINUTE=240
# GHOST_MARKET_REPORTS_A_DAY=500
# GHOST_MARKET_NEED_VERIFIED=0
# GHOST_MARKET_REVIEW_KEYS=

# --- Files spaces' limits --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional; each has a default where unset (relay/lib/files.dart FilesConfig). Bytes for sizes. The defaults allow
# 50 GB files and 300 GB in all: lower GHOST_FILES_TOTAL to fit your disk. GHOST_FILES_MAX_BODY is held to 10 MiB, so
# a web server in front must let a 10 MiB request body through (the sample Caddy and nginx configs do).
# GHOST_FILES_MAX_FILE=50004194304
# GHOST_FILES_SPACE_QUOTA=50000000000
# GHOST_FILES_ROOMY_QUOTA=256000000000
# GHOST_FILES_ROOMY_SPACES=
# GHOST_FILES_TOTAL=400000000000
# GHOST_FILES_SPACES_PER_ID=20
# GHOST_FILES_IDLE_DAYS=90
# GHOST_FILES_WRITES_PER_MINUTE=600
# GHOST_FILES_MAX_BODY=8388608
# GHOST_FILES_MAX_OP=32768
# GHOST_FILES_MAX_OPS=20000
# GHOST_FILES_MAX_LOG_BYTES=16777216
# GHOST_FILES_MAX_BLOBS=10000
# GHOST_FILES_MAX_SPACES=100000
# GHOST_FILES_PART_HOURS=24
# GHOST_FILES_SPACES_PER_ADDRESS=20
# GHOST_FILES_PROMISED_PER_ADDRESS=60000000000

# --- Verifying phone numbers by text (Twilio Verify) -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional. Without all three, nobody can verify a number on your relay (so number-based lookups and invites by
# number find nobody there). Your own Twilio account: the account or API key SID, its token, and a Verify service SID.
# GHOST_TEXT_TO is the country codes a code may be texted to, commas between (+1); GHOST_TEXTS_A_DAY caps them (200).
# GHOST_TWILIO_SID=
# GHOST_TWILIO_TOKEN=
# GHOST_TWILIO_VERIFY=
# GHOST_TEXT_TO=+1
# GHOST_TEXTS_A_DAY=200

# --- Linking accounts in Settings › Me (Discord, X, Telegram, GitHub) ------------------------------------------------
# Optional; a network left empty is "not set up" in the app. Each needs your own app registered with that network,
# with GHOST_PUBLIC_URL/link/... as its callback. Discord's and Telegram's come in pairs.
# GHOST_DISCORD_ID=
# GHOST_DISCORD_SECRET=
# GHOST_X_ID=
# GHOST_X_SECRET=
# GHOST_TELEGRAM_BOT=
# GHOST_TELEGRAM_TOKEN=
# GitHub works without a token, but reads far less often:
# GHOST_GITHUB_TOKEN=

# --- Things only Uplink's own relay can use -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Listed so the list is complete. Leave them empty on your relay.
# Waking a closed iPhone needs Apple's push key for the app's own bundle id, which only the app's publisher holds:
# on your relay iPhones are not woken while the app is closed (they get what was held when they next open it).
# GHOST_APNS_KEY_FILE=
# GHOST_APNS_KEY_ID=
# GHOST_APNS_TEAM_ID=
# GHOST_APNS_TOPIC=
# The feedback board (the app's Share feedback, filed in a Notion database): both, or neither.
# GHOST_NOTION_TOKEN=
# GHOST_NOTION_FEEDBACK=
# The bug-bounty key's public half (33 bytes in hex).
# GHOST_BOUNTY_KEY=
